String Class Constructors in Java
In Java, the String class provides several constructors to create and initialize string objects in different ways. Here's a summary of the most commonly used constructors:

1. String()
Creates an empty string "".
String s1 = new String();
System.out.println(s1); // Output: (empty string)

 2. String(String original)
Creates a new string that is a copy of an existing string.
String s2 = new String("Hello");
System.out.println(s2); // Output: Hello

3. String(char[] value)
Converts a char array to a string.
char[] ch = {'J', 'A', 'V', 'A'};
String s3 = new String(ch);
System.out.println(s3); // Output: JAVA

4. String(char[] value, int offset, int count)
Creates a string from a sub-array of characters.

 5. String(byte[] bytes)
Converts a byte array to string using platform's default charset.
byte[] b = {65, 66, 67};
String s5 = new String(b);
System.out.println(s5); // Output: ABC

6. String(byte[] bytes, int offset, int length)
Creates a string from a sub-array of bytes.
byte[] b = {65, 66, 67, 68, 69};
String s6 = new String(b, 1, 3);
System.out.println(s6); // Output: BCD

7. String(StringBuffer buffer)
Creates a new string from a StringBuffer.
StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer("Hello");
String s7 = new String(sb);
System.out.println(s7); // Output: Hello

8. String(StringBuilder builder)
Creates a new string from a StringBuilder.
StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("World");
String s8 = new String(sb);
System.out.println(s8); // Output: World

